---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Definition = `THE DIGITAL DIVIDE

  differing access to computing devices and
  the Internet, based on

    socioeconomic
    geographic
    demographic

  characteristics

Not "some people don't have the internet."
Access that varies, and varies with those things.`;

const Chain = `FROM SESSION 66

  "Access to the Internet depends on the ability
   to connect a computing device to an
   Internet-connected device."

Access is a CHAIN. It ends where the chain ends.`;

const Four = `IT AFFECTS

  GROUPS       and       INDIVIDUALS

IT RAISES ISSUES OF

  equity      access      influence

BOTH

  globally    and    locally`;

const Actions = `AND IT IS NOT WEATHER

  "The digital divide is affected by the actions of
   individuals, organizations, and governments."

Somebody's decisions widen it.
Somebody's decisions narrow it.`;

const NineHours = `WHY THIS COURSE WORKS THE WAY IT DOES

  The College Board requires a MINIMUM OF 9 HOURS
  of class time for the Create Performance Task.

  Why in class, and not as homework?

  Because a student without a computer or internet
  at home would otherwise be unable to do it.

You spent ten sessions on that task.
That was a digital divide policy.`;

const Local = `LOOK CLOSER THAN "COUNTRIES"

  Two students in the same school:

    one has a laptop and fast broadband at home
    one shares a phone with two siblings

  Same country. Same town. Same classroom.
  Same assignment due tomorrow.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.12: The Digital Divide</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>One Definition, Precisely</h2>

          <p>
            <b>This is a short topic with one learning objective and five statements</b>,
            and it is asked about constantly &mdash; because it is the clearest example
            of a computing effect that lands on people unevenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">It is also the reason this class is arranged the
            way it is</b>, which is not something you can say about most topics on an
            exam.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.12</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">The Digital Divide</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Differing Access, and What It Varies With</p>
              <p>Groups and Individuals, Globally and Locally</p>
              <p>Somebody's Actions</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Definition</h2>
              <Code code={Definition} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Say All Three Words" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Three Words to Keep</h2>
              <p><b>Socioeconomic</b> &mdash; money, and what money buys.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Geographic</b> &mdash; where you are.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Demographic</b> &mdash; age, and other characteristics of a population.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The exam lists all three. <b>An answer with one of them is a third of an answer.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You Already Have the Mechanism</h2>
              <Code code={Chain} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Session 66" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Infrastructure has to reach you before access is even a question.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Four Things It Is Not Only</h2>
              <Code code={Four} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Both Sides of Each" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b>Individuals, not only groups. Locally, not only globally.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Local Half Is the One People Skip</h2>
              <Code code={Local} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Same Classroom" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">The divide is not a map. <b>It is in this room.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Influence Is the Third Word</h2>
              <p>Equity and access are the obvious two.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b>Influence</b> is the one worth thinking about: if a policy is
                announced online, who hears it?
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">Who gets consulted? Whose complaints get counted?</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>It Is Not Weather</h2>
              <Code code={Actions} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Somebody Decided" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b>Individuals. Organizations. Governments.</b> All three are named.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Means It Can Be Narrowed</h2>
              <p>Free public wireless. Loaned devices. Classes for people who have access and not the training.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Infrastructure programs reaching unserved communities.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>None of those are inevitable. Somebody chose them.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Including in Here</h2>
              <Code code={NineHours} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Nine Hours" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And the Optional Sessions</h2>
              <p>This class offers time outside lessons to work on your programs.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b class="text-sn-theme">That is an individual's action affecting the
                digital divide.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">Which is IOC-1.C.5, and you are standing in it.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Defining the digital divide with all three characteristics</li>
                <li>Explaining that it affects individuals as well as groups</li>
                <li>Explaining that it operates locally as well as globally</li>
                <li>Naming the issues it raises: equity, access, influence</li>
                <li>Explaining how individuals, organizations and governments affect it</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Digital divide</dt>
                  <dd>Differing access to computing devices and the internet, based on socioeconomic, geographic or demographic characteristics.</dd>
                  <dt>Equity</dt>
                  <dd>Whether people are given what they need to have a fair chance &mdash; not whether they are given the same thing.</dd>
                  <dt>Influence</dt>
                  <dd>Whose voice reaches a decision. The issue the digital divide raises that is easiest to miss.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Which Characteristic?</h3>

          <p>
            For each situation, say whether the access gap is mainly
            <b> socioeconomic</b>, <b>geographic</b>, or <b>demographic</b> &mdash; and
            note where more than one applies.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>A village forty miles from the nearest fiber line</li>
            <li>A family that cannot afford a monthly broadband bill</li>
            <li>An eighty-year-old who has a tablet and has never been shown how to use it</li>
            <li>A student who shares one phone with two siblings</li>
            <li>A country whose government restricts which sites can be reached</li>
            <li>A neighborhood where providers chose not to build because too few people would subscribe</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Item 6 is the interesting one.</b> Which characteristic is it, and which
            of the three actors &mdash; individuals, organizations, governments &mdash;
            made the decision?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Groups and Individuals, Globally and Locally</h3>

          <p><b>Four boxes. Fill in a real example for each.</b></p>

          <table class="table table-sm align-middle mt-3">
            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Affecting a group</th><th>Affecting an individual</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><b>Globally</b></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>Locally</b></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The local-individual box is the one most students
            leave vague.</b> Make it specific enough that somebody could be helped by
            it.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Equity, Access, and Influence</h3>

          <p>
            <b>A town council announces road closures only on a social media
            account.</b>
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>Describe the <b>access</b> problem in one sentence.</li>
            <li>Describe the <b>equity</b> problem. How is it different from the access problem?</li>
            <li>
              Describe the <b>influence</b> problem. <b>Who ends up with less say in
              what the council does</b>, and by what mechanism?
            </li>
            <li><b>Propose one change</b> the council could make. Say which of the three problems it fixes and which it does not.</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Question 4 is the graded one.</b> A change that fixes all three is
            usually a change nobody could afford.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: This Class</h3>

          <p>
            The College Board requires <b>at least nine hours of class time</b> for the
            Create Performance Task rather than letting it be homework.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li><b>Explain why</b>, using the definition of the digital divide.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Who would have been disadvantaged</b> if the task had been set as
              homework? Name the characteristic.
            </li>
            <li>
              This class also offers optional sessions outside lesson time. <b>Which of
              the three actors is that</b>, and what does it fix?
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Name one thing about this course that still assumes access you might
              not have.</b> Be honest; this is a real question.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Question 4 is the one worth answering
            carefully</b>, and the answers get read.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Define the digital divide, including <b>all three</b> characteristics.</li>
            <li>Does it affect only groups?</li>
            <li>Does it exist only between countries?</li>
            <li>Name the three issues it raises.</li>
            <li>Name the three kinds of actor whose actions affect it.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>&quot;Access&quot; and &quot;equity&quot; are different problems. Explain the difference using an example where somebody has access and still does not have equity.</li>
            <li>The digital divide is affected by the actions of individuals. Name one action a single person could take that would narrow it for one other person.</li>
            <li>Session 66 said access depends on connecting to an internet-connected device. Explain how that one sentence predicts most of this session.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>All four tasks, plus your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
